  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 PM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nMostly clear skies remain in place this afternoon, and these \nconditions are expected to persist through tonight. The only other \nitem of note in the near-term is that wind will continue to shift \nthrough the evening and overnight time frame to become out of the \nsoutheast. Once wind completes the shift, gusts will increase to 20 \nto 30 miles per hour at their peak, expanding from central and north \ncentral South Dakota today to the entire forecast area by Friday \nmorning. \n\nA low-level jet develops Friday evening, which when combined with a \nshortwave aloft will help develop storms over the area. There is \nalso some consensus on the presence of a warm front oriented west to \neast, extending from a low pressure center that will move into \nwestern South Dakota. There is still some disagreement within the \nmodels as to the north/south placement and progression of this \nfront, which leads to some additional uncertainty on the location of \nstorm development. High-resolution models tend to show a stronger \nsignal for storms to move in from the west, influenced more by the \nshortwave energy and low pressure center. A smaller subset of models \nalso show the potential for development along the warm front and low-\nlevel jet over northeastern South Dakota. With both areas of storms \nin the evening, the expectations are that hail and wind will be the \nprimary severe threats. 1000-2000 J/kg of MUCAPE will be in place, \nand shear of 30-40 knots will support the hail potential, along with \nmid-level lapse rates between 7 and 8 C/km. Additionally, DCAPE \nvalues over 1000 J/kg are expected over much of the area, leading to \nthe wind potential, particularly if stronger winds from the low-\nlevel jet are able to mix to the surface. Not anticipating a \nwidespread tornado threat at this time, but can't entirely rule it \nout either. If a brief spinup were to occur, the most likely \nlocation would be along the warm front. \n\nAnother shortwave aloft and associated vorticity maximum is set to \nmove into the region Saturday evening, thereby creating another \nchance for storm development. Once again it appears as though hail \nwill be the dominant threat due to MUCAPE of 1000-2000 J/kg coupled \nwith 40-50 knots of shear. Mid-level lapse rates are generally \nexpected to fall in the 6-7 C/km range, which is perhaps a little \nless favorable than ideal conditions for development, but still \nplenty steep enough to sustain the hail threat. Confidence is a bit \nlower in the severe wind threat, mainly due to a low signal for \nanything more than isolated pockets of DCAPE > 1000 J/kg. However \nwith low-level lapse rates near dry adiabatic, stronger gusts and/or \nmicrobursts may occasionally produce a severe gust or two. With this \nthreat, there is another Marginal Risk for severe weather in place, \nmainly covering north central and parts of central South Dakota. \n\nStarting Sunday and into next week, the broad upper-level ridge that \nhas been in place will shift eastward. As a result, an upper-level \nlow will pass to the north, bringing multiple jet streaks over the \nregion through early next week. This setup will be conducive to \nadditional rain chances, with the best chances generally located \nalong the North Dakota/South Dakota border. Still a bit early to \ntalk about severe weather potential, and medium-range machine \nlearning models do not give a strong signal for severe potential \nafter Saturday.",
